= Majority-label bias =
== Overview ==
'''Majority-label bias''' is a reported in-context-learning tendency for a language model's predictions to be influenced by the frequency of labels demonstrated in the prompt.

== In-context learning ==
When demonstrations contain an imbalanced label distribution, models may favor labels that appear more frequently even when that frequency is not relevant to the target item.

== Measurement caution ==
Apparent majority-label effects can interact with option order, token properties, task complexity, and prompt format. They should therefore be separated experimentally from [[Positional bias (language model)]].

== References ==
* Zhao et al., “Calibrate Before Use: Improving Few-Shot Performance of Language Models”, ICML 2021.
* Mina et al., “Cognitive Biases, Task Complexity, and Result Interpretability in Large Language Models”, COLING 2025.

